-- title:
--   Model-fixing coordinate changes versus rational automorphisms preserving ⟨ g⟩
-- statement:
--   Let $\kappa$ be an algebraically closed field, let $M'$ be a nonzero natural number whose image in $\kappa$ is nonzero, and let $W$ be an elliptic Weierstrass curve over $\kappa$. Let $T$ assign to each prime factor $p$ of $M'$ a polynomial $T_p \in \kappa[X]$ satisfying [`ModularCurve.IsGamma0PowAt`](def/ModularCurve_WeierstrassGamma0Pow.html#L55) for $p$ and $k = v_p(M')$: if $p^k = 2$ this means $\deg T_p \le 1$, the coefficient of $X$ is $1$, and $T_p \mid W.\Psi_2^2$; otherwise it means $\deg T_p \le \varphi(p^k)/2$, the coefficient in degree $\varphi(p^k)/2$ is $1$, $T_p \cdot \mathrm{pre}\Psi_{p^{k-1}} \mid \mathrm{pre}\Psi_{p^k}$, and $T_p$ divides `W.smulNumerator a (φ(p^k)/2) T_p` for every $a$ with $2 \le a \le (p^k-1)/2$ and $p \nmid a$. Let $g$ be an affine point of $W$ of exact additive order $M'$, linked to $T$ in the sense that whenever $n \cdot g$ is an affine point $(x_1,y_1)$ of exact order $p^{v_p(M')}$, $x_1$ is a root of $T_p$. The conclusion equates two cardinalities: that of the set of Weierstrass variable changes $C$ with $C \bullet W = W$ and $C(u^{-2d})\, T_p(u^2X + r) = T_p$ for all $p$, where $d = 1$ if $p^k = 2$ and $d = \varphi(p^k)/2$ otherwise; and that of the set of additive endomorphisms $\iota$ of $W(\kappa)$ lying in [`WeierstrassCurve.rationalHomSet κ W W`](def/WeierstrassCurve_RationalEnd.html#L28) (zero, or given off a finite set of $x$-coordinates by ratios of bivariate polynomials over $\kappa$), admitting a two-sided inverse again in that set, and with $\iota(\langle g\rangle) = \langle g\rangle$ for the subgroup of integer multiples of $g$.
--
--   This is the dictionary between admissible coordinate changes preserving a Weierstrass model together with a $\Gamma_0$-type tuple of kernel polynomials, and rationally represented automorphisms of the underlying pointed elliptic curve preserving the cyclic subgroup generated by $g$. It feeds the automorphism count for the full-level moduli problem, being used in [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.natCard_variableChange_act_curve_eq_and_level_fst_eq_eq_two_mul_placeWidthChar_of_over_of_eq_map_classify_rigidDataPow_of_tatePoint`](thm.html#ModularCurve.FullLevel.natCard_variableChange_act_curve_eq_and_level_fst_eq_eq_two_mul_placeWidthChar_of_over_of_eq_map_classify_rigidDataPow_of_tatePoint).
